## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design a runtime-extensible, order-sensitive weaving system with per-module isolation, robust failure handling, and a practical TypeScript usage pattern.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic proxy wrapping around a service
- Runtime registry for concerns by name
- Ordered execution with per-module scoping
- Safe error isolation and fault tolerance
- Trade-offs: dependency injection's explicit wiring versus AOP's seamless weaving

## Code Example

```typescript
interface IConcern {
  name: string;
  before(ctx: ConcernContext): void | Promise<void>;
  after?(ctx: ConcernContext, result: unknown): void | Promise<void>;
  around?(ctx: ConcernContext, next: () => Promise<unknown>): Promise<unknown>;
}

interface ConcernContext {
  method: string;
  args: unknown[];
  module: string;
  [key: string]: unknown;
}

class ConcernRegistry {
  private concerns = new Map<string, Map<string, IConcern>>();

  register(module: string, concern: IConcern): void {
    if (!this.concerns.has(module)) {
      this.concerns.set(module, new Map());
    }
    this.concerns.get(module)!.set(concern.name, concern);
  }

  toggle(module: string, name: string, enabled: boolean): void {
    const moduleConcerns = this.concerns.get(module);
    if (moduleConcerns?.has(name)) {
      moduleConcerns.get(name)!["enabled"] = enabled;
    }
  }

  getOrdered(module: string, order: string[]): IConcern[] {
    const moduleConcerns = this.concerns.get(module) || new Map();
    return order
      .map(name => moduleConcerns.get(name))
      .filter((c): c is IConcern => c !== undefined && (c as any)["enabled"] !== false);
  }
}

function wrap<T>(target: T, module: string, registry: ConcernRegistry, order: string[]): T {
  return new Proxy(target, {
    async get(_target, prop) {
      const member = (target as any)[prop];
      if (typeof member !== "function") return member;

      return async (...args: unknown[]) => {
        const ctx: ConcernContext = { method: prop as string, args, module };
        const concerns = registry.getOrdered(module, order);

        const pipeline = concerns.reduce(
          (next, concern) => async () => {
            try {
              await concern.before?.(ctx);
              const result = await next();
              await concern.after?.(ctx, result);
              return result;
            } catch (e) {
              console.error(`Concern ${concern.name} failed:`, e);
              return next();
            }
          },
          () => member.apply(target, args)
        );

        return pipeline();
      };
    },
  }) as T;
}

// Usage
const loggingConcern: IConcern = {
  name: "logging",
  before: (ctx) => console.log(`Calling ${ctx.method}`),
  after: (ctx, result) => console.log(`Finished ${ctx.method}`),
};

const metricsConcern: IConcern = {
  name: "metrics",
  before: (ctx) => { (ctx as any).start = Date.now(); },
  after: (ctx) => { console.log(`Duration: ${Date.now() - (ctx as any).start}ms`); },
};

const registry = new ConcernRegistry();
registry.register("users", loggingConcern);
registry.register("users", metricsConcern);

const service = { getUser: (id: number) => `User ${id}` };
const wrapped = wrap(service, "users", registry, ["logging", "metrics"]);

wrapped.getUser(1); // Logs: Calling getUser, Duration: ..., Finished getUser
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores runtime extensibility and undoable actions through a practical pattern common in tooling and scripting domains.

## Key Concepts
- **Command pattern** with undo support
- **Runtime plugin registry** for lazy loading
- **Safe macro/sequence composition** with transactional semantics
- **Isolation and error handling** for plugins

## Code Example
```typescript
// types.ts
export interface Context {
  args: string[]
  env: Record<string, string>
}

export interface Command {
  name: string
  execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
  undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void>
}

export type CommandFactory = (name: string) => Command | Promise<Command>

// registry.ts
export class Registry {
  private factories = new Map<string, CommandFactory>()
  private commands = new Map<string, Command>()

  register(name: string, factory: CommandFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory)
  }

  async get(name: string): Promise<Command> {
    if (!this.commands.has(name)) {
      const factory = this.factories.get(name)
      if (!factory) throw new Error(`Command "${name}" not found`)
      this.commands.set(name, await factory(name))
    }
    return this.commands.get(name)!
  }
}

// macro.ts
export class Macro implements Command {
  name: string
  private commands: Command[]

  constructor(name: string, commands: Command[]) {
    this.name = name
    this.commands = commands
  }

  async execute(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    const executed: Command[] = []
    try {
      for (const cmd of this.commands) {
        await cmd.execute(ctx)
        executed.push(cmd)
      }
    } catch (err) {
      for (const cmd of executed.reverse()) {
        await cmd.undo(ctx)
      }
      throw err
    }
  }

  async undo(ctx: Context): Promise<void> {
    for (const cmd of this.commands.reverse()) {
      await cmd.undo(ctx)
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests knowledge of runtime extensibility, per-endpoint configuration, and safe concurrency control in distributed systems. The candidate must justify pattern choices, discuss trade-offs in dynamic environments, and demonstrate awareness of deadlock prevention and backpressure strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for encapsulating concurrency behavior
- Registry + Factory pattern for runtime-extensible policy injection
- Per-endpoint isolation with sensible defaults
- Deadlock avoidance through careful resource acquisition ordering and bounded queues
- Backpressure considerations to prevent memory exhaustion under load

## Code Example
```typescript
interface ConcurrencyPolicy {
  submit(job: () => void | Promise<void>): void;
  start(): void;
  stop(): void;
}

interface PolicyFactory {
  create(config?: Record<string, unknown>): ConcurrencyPolicy;
}

class PolicyRegistry {
  private factories = new Map<string, PolicyFactory>();

  register(name: string, factory: PolicyFactory): void {
    this.factories.set(name, factory);
  }

  create(name: string, config?: Record<string, unknown>): ConcurrencyPolicy {
    const factory = this.factories.get(name);
    if (!factory) throw new Error(`Unknown policy: ${name}`);
    return factory.create(config);
  }
}

// Built-in policies
class FixedWorkerPool implements ConcurrencyPolicy {
  private queue: Array<() => void> = [];
  private active = 0;

  constructor(private maxWorkers: number) {}

  submit(job: () => void): void {
    if (this.active < this.maxWorkers) {
      this.active++;
      job();
    } else {
      this.queue.push(job);
    }
  }

  start(): void { /* drain queue */ }
  stop(): void { /* reject queued */ }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PolicyRegistry();
registry.register('fixed', (config) => new FixedWorkerPool(config?.workers ?? 10));

const runner = new TaskRunner(registry);
runner.assign('POST /orders', 'fixed', { workers: 20 });
```

## Trade-off Discussion
| Aspect | FixedWorkerPool | WorkStealingPool | BandwidthLimitedPool |
|--------|-----------------|------------------|----------------------|
| Throughput | Predictable, bounded | High under skew | Rate-limited |
| Latency | Moderate queue delay | Low for varied work | Controlled but variable |
| Complexity | Low | High (deques, synchronization) | Medium (token bucket) |
| Deadlock Risk | Low if bounded queue | Medium (circular stealing) | Low |
| Memory | Bounded by queue size | Unbounded steals possible | Bounded by token rate |

